nt expenditure - NET REVENUE EXPENDITURE (TOTAL OF LINES 800 TO 804)","form":"RS","row":"805","col":"1"}}]}],"context":[{"label":"Resident population","value":98427,"year":"2020 est.","kind":"int"},{"label":"GVA (balanced, total)","value":1882000.0,"year":"2023","kind":"money"},{"label":"GVA per head","value":19648,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"GDP per head","value":22618,"year":"2023","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D council tax (area bill)","value":2338.21,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"},{"label":"Band D \u2014 council's own element","value":191.5,"year":"2026-27","kind":"gbp"}]}},"dossier":{"summary":null,"compliance":null,"documents":[],"read_documents":[],"calendar":null,"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"The council is deliberately using internal borrowing from cash reserves to avoid expensive medium- to long-term external debt while rates remain high.","lead":"Internal borrowing depletes the council's liquid reserves and creates refinancing risk if rates do not fall, potentially forcing expensive external borrowing later.","iso":"2026-07-31","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Investment income is projected to fall \u00a3100k short of budget as higher rates are offset by lower cash balances from internal borrowing.","lead":"Lower-than-expected investment returns reduce the council's capacity to self-fund services and increase dependency on borrowing or reserves.","iso":"2026-07-31","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"External borrowing has increased from \u00a312.1m to forecast \u00a317m to fund the capital programme.","lead":"A significant single-year increase in external debt changes the council's leverage and debt service costs, material to any lending decision.","iso":"2026-07-31","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-07-31","committee":"Audit & Accounts Committee","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","headline":"The council is deliberately using internal borrowing from cash reserves to avoid expensive medium- to long-term external debt while rates remain high.","detail":"Rather than locking into long-term PWLB loans at current elevated rates (5.00% for 5-year, 5.50% for 10-year), the council is funding capital spending from spare cash balances and only taking short-term external borrowing. This strategy depends on rates falling to avoid eventually taking out expensive long-term debt.","category":"borrowing","matters":"Internal borrowing depletes the council's liquid reserves and creates refinancing risk if rates do not fall, potentially forcing expensive external borrowing later.","figures":"5yr PWLB rate: 5.00%. 10yr PWLB rate: 5.50%. Short-term loan rate: 4.25% (6 months).","quote":"PWLB and intra-local authority external borrowing interest rates are also expected to be higher than at budget setting, which continues to restrict the opportunity to take medium to long term external debt. The practice continues to avoid medium to long term borrowing commitments by utilising spare cash balances as internal funding, or short-term borrowing with a view to reviewing borrowing requirements should the rates begin to decrease.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-31","committee":"Audit & Accounts Committee","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","headline":"Investment income is projected to fall \u00a3100k short of budget as higher rates are offset by lower cash balances from internal borrowing.","detail":"The council budgeted to receive \u00a3626,130 in investment income in 2026/27, assuming interest rates would fall. Whilst rates have remained higher, the investment portfolio has shrunk due to internal borrowing and cash flow fluctuations, resulting in a net shortfall of \u00a3100k.","category":"income","matters":"Lower-than-expected investment returns reduce the council's capacity to self-fund services and increase dependency on borrowing or reserves.","figures":"Budgeted investment income: \u00a3626,130. Forecast shortfall: \u00a3100,000. Average portfolio balance: \u00a313.8m. Quarter-end portfolio: \u00a314.8m.","quote":"The Council has budgeted to receive \u00a3626,130 in investment income in 2026/27 based on the expectation of reducing interest rates over the year. Whilst interest rates have remained higher so far in the year, the size of investment portfolio is impact by continued internal borrowing and other cash flow fluctuations. A shortfall of \u00a3100,000 is forecast against this budget but is subject to change based on these factors throughout the year.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-31","committee":"Audit & Accounts Committee","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","headline":"External borrowing has increased from \u00a312.1m to forecast \u00a317m to fund the capital programme.","detail":"The council took a new \u00a35m loan in April 2026 at 4.25% for 6 months and is forecasting year-end borrowing of \u00a317m. This 40% increase funds the capital programme net financing need of \u00a311.3m. All borrowing remains within the operational boundary (\u00a333.5m) and authorised limit (\u00a335.5m).","category":"borrowing","matters":"A significant single-year increase in external debt changes the council's leverage and debt service costs, material to any lending decision.","figures":"Opening borrowing: \u00a312.1m. New loan: \u00a35m at 4.25% (6 months). Forecast year-end: \u00a317m. Operational boundary: \u00a333.501m. Authorised limit: \u00a335.501m.","quote":"The Council's external borrowing outstanding at 31 March 2026 was \u00a312.1million. A new loan of \u00a35million was taken in April at 4.25% for 6 months. Borrowing at the end of the year is forecast to be \u00a317million but remains under review to support the general fund underlying borrowing need.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-31","committee":"Audit & Accounts Committee","paper_title":"SM 2627 Q1 TM Update App A 21-07-26","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53539/53539.pdf","headline":"Interest rates have not fallen as budget assumed, affecting both borrowing costs and investment returns.","detail":"The budget was set expecting the Bank of England base rate to fall to 3.50% by end of Q1 and 3.25% by end of Q3. The latest forecast shows no decrease \u2014 rates remain at 3.75% for the whole financial year. PWLB rates are also higher than anticipated, restricting external borrowing opportunities.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A sustained higher interest rate environment increases the council's debt servicing costs and reduces investment returns, directly squeezing the general fund budget.","figures":"Base rate: 3.75% (no change expected). Budgeted rate decreases: to 3.50% (Q1), then 3.25% (Q3). 5yr PWLB: 5.00%. 10yr PWLB: 5.50%.","quote":"The latest forecast does not expect any further decrease in this financial year. The budget was set with the assumption that there would be two decreases during the year, to 3.50% by the end of quarter 1 and 3.25% by the end of quarter 3.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-07-31","committee":"Audit & Accounts Committee","paper_title":"Internal Audit Annual Report","paper_url":"https://democracy.staffsmoorlands.gov.uk/documents/s53565/53565.pdf","headline":"Four service areas received limited assurance ratings for internal controls","detail":"Internal Audit assessed control effectiveness across 32 audited service areas. Four service areas received 'limited assurance' ratings, meaning weaknesses in control systems or non-compliance with controls put achievement of system objectives at risk. Two areas achieved substantial assurance and 26 achieved satisfactory assurance; zero areas received unsatisfactory ratings.","category":"governance","matters":"Control weaknesses identified across multiple service areas suggest potential gaps in risk management and financial oversight that could affect council operations and creditworthiness","figures":"4 service areas limited assurance, 26 satisfactory assurance, 2 substantial assurance, 0 unsatisfactory assurance","quote":"4 service areas had controls within the system to provide limited assurance that risks material to the achievement of the systems objectives are adequately managed","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-07-31"},"name":"Staffordshire Moorlands","findings_total":34,"news_drafts_total":3}}